{
  "title": "Proximal Humerus Fracture - Pediatric",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4004/proximal-humerus-fracture--pediatric",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Apr 24 2024",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Proximal Humerus Fractures are relatively common physeal and metaphyseal fractures of the proximal humerus.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is confirmed with plain radiographs of the shoulder.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is usually nonoperative in younger patients due to the remodeling potential of the proximal humerus. Operative management is indicated for significant displacement in older children with minimal physeal growth remaining.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "< 5% of fractures in children",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "most common in adolescents (**peak age at 15 years**), but may occur in younger patients",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anatomical location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Salter-Harris classification**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "SH-I is most frequent in <5 year olds",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "SH-II is most frequent in >12 year olds",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**metaphyseal fractures**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "typically occur in 5 to 12 year olds",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism of injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "blunt trauma",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "indirect trauma",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy of physeal fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "**proximal fragment (epiphysis) displacement**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "abducted and externally rotated due to rotator cuff muscles",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "**distal fragment (shaft) displacement**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "anterior, adducted and shortened due to pectoralis major and deltoid muscles",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographic appearance of secondary ossification centers",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**proximal humeral epiphysis at** 6 mos",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**greater tuberosity** appears at 1-3 yrs",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**lesser tuberosity** appears at 4-5 yrs",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Growth",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "proximal humerus physis closes at 14-17 in girls, 16-18 in boys",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**80% of humerus growth comes from the proximal physis**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "highest proximal:distal ratio difference (femur is second with 30:70 proximal:distal ratio)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Contributes to high remodeling potential",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "",
